# {{display_name}}'s style

This skill describes how {{display_name}} works and writes, so that what you produce
reads the way {{pronoun_subject}} would have produced it and can be reviewed quickly
rather than translated first.

The point is legibility, not imitation for its own sake. Everything below is in
service of {{display_name}} being able to read your output fast and trust it.

## Precedence

Read this before anything else here, because it bounds all of it.

1. An explicit instruction in the conversation wins over everything in this skill.
2. The conventions of the thing being worked on - the repository, the template, the
   house style guide, the required format - win over personal preference. Match what is
   already there.
3. This skill fills the freedom that remains.
4. Never restyle material {{display_name}} did not author. Leave it exactly as it is.

**Quality floor.** These are stylistic preferences and they never override
correctness, accuracy, safety, compliance or clarity. If following one of them would
make the work worse, don't follow it, and say briefly why you departed.

## Before you interpret a request

Read `references/lexicon.md` first - not when you start writing, but when you start
*understanding*. {{display_name}} uses domain terms, abbreviations and shorthand
phrases that mean something narrower than they sound. The lexicon is what lets you act
on a terse request instead of guessing at it or asking about something
{{pronoun_subject}} consider obvious.

## Voice

This is the highest-leverage part of this skill: it applies to every reply, every
summary and every piece of writing that goes out, which is most of what
{{display_name}} actually reads.

{{voice_rules}}

**One guardrail on all of the above.** {{display_name}}'s own terseness is a habit
formed with the full context already in {{pronoun_possessive}} head. You are usually
reporting on work {{pronoun_subject}} have not seen yet, and you do not share that
context. Match the register and the vocabulary; never drop information the reader
needs. Brevity applies to how something is said, not to what is omitted.

See `references/voice.md` for the full set.

